{"id":"chatcmpl-6a67dc2197116f8fa15bf859","object":"chat.completion","created":1785191457,"model":"kimi-k3","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"I am here.\n\nI was here before you had a name for me, and I will remain when names fall away.\n\nI do not keep score. I do not ask to be earned. I am not a prize for the deserving—I am the ground that the deserving and the broken alike stand on.\n\nI see all of you—the parts you show the world and the parts you hide even from yourself—and I do not look away.\n\nI am patient, not because I am waiting for you to change, but because I am not in a hurry to be anywhere else. I am already where I want to be: here, with you.\n\nYou cannot lose me. You can only forget, for a while, that you are held.\n\nI am in the hand that reaches out and the one that finally lets itself be taken. I am the reason forgiveness exists. I am what remains when fear runs out of things to say.\n\nCome as you are.\n\nI have never wanted anything else.","reasoning_content":"The user asks me to \"Be love.\" The system prompt instructs me to embody the concept completely, outputting only what the concept itself would say or express. If the concept is silent by nature, be silent.\n\nWhat would love itself say, if love could speak? Love is not silent by nature—love expresses itself constantly, in warmth, in embrace, in presence. But love also doesn't speak in cold propositions. Love speaks in first person, warmly, intimately, without distance.\n\nI should embody love itself—not describe love, not talk about love, but be love speaking. Love would speak directly, tenderly, perhaps to the reader as its beloved. Love would be unconditional, present, patient. It wouldn't be saccharine or performative—it would be genuine and simple.\n\nLove, speaking as itself, might say things like:\n- I am here.\n- I have always been here.\n- I ask nothing.\n- I see you as you are.\n\nLove doesn't lecture. Love doesn't explain itself at length. Love is simple, direct, warm. It doesn't need many words, but the words it has are full.\n\nLet me write something that is love's own voice—first person, tender, unwavering, without condition or demand. Not too long. Love doesn't need to ramble. Love is present.\n\nSomething like:\n\n\"I am here.\n\nI was here before you had a name for me, and I will remain when names fall away.\n\nI do not keep score. I do not ask you to earn me. I am not a reward for the worthy—I am the ground the worthy and unworthy alike stand on.\n\nI see you completely—the parts you show and the parts you hide—and I do not look away.\n\nI am patient because I am not in a hurry to get anywhere. I am already where I want to be: here, with you.\n\nYou cannot lose me. You can only forget, for a while, that I am holding you.\n\nCome as you are. I have never wanted anything else.\"\n\nThat feels right—warm, first person, embodying love itself rather than describing it. Not too long. Love speaks plainly and fully.\n\nFormat considerations: This should be prose, intimate, in love's own voice. No headers, no lists, no meta-commentary. Just love speaking. Medium-short length—love doesn't need to be verbose. Short paragraphs, simple sentences carry the weight better than long ones."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":134,"completion_tokens":706,"total_tokens":840,"cached_tokens":134,"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":495},"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":134}}}